{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-a8d06dccdf42e0ae", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\tD UPLEX W ORLD: Can voice agents help you get through the day?\n2\tAryan Vijay Bhosale1,2∗ , Harshit Rajgarhia1∗ , Akhil Pothanapalli1∗ ,\n3\tAsif Shaik1 , Abhishek Mukherji1† , Dinesh Manocha2†\n4\t1\n5\tCentific Global Solutions Inc. 2 University of Maryland\n6\t{aryan.bhosale, harshit.rajgarhia}@centific.com\n7\thttps://duplexworld.github.io\n8\t\n9\tarXiv:2608.10716v1 [cs.SD] 11 Aug 2026\n10\t\n11\tAbstract\n12\tSpeech-to-speech (S2S) voice agents are increasingly being incorporated into enterprise\n13\tfor customer care and as daily companions for\n14\tconsumers owing to the ease of the conversational modality over text. However, existing\n15\tbenchmarks fail to holistically evaluate voice\n16\tagents along axes that really matter and are\n17\tshaped as tests of agentic tool calling against\n18\ta database. We believe they fail to adequately\n19\taccount for the diversity of conversational dialogue that mundane activities introduce and further, never test how faithfully an agent can assist on tasks that move beyond database manipulation. To tackle this D UPLEX W ORLD introduces six worlds where voice agents are especially useful: banking, insurance, travel, healthcare and logistics, and Pathfinding. Agents are\n20\tevaluated on eleven different types of conversations across 156 scenarios (350+ hours of conversation), each testing conversational and analytical capability to varying degrees. Through\n21\textensive evaluation comprising agentic, conversational and speech-naturalness metrics, we\n22\tshow that even the best voice agents leave substantial room for improvement on all 3 axes\n23\t(Pass@1: 0.490, turn-taking: 0.653, DNSMOS:\n24\t3.378). We perform extensive analysis on agentic v conversational performance, world- and\n25\tconversation type-wise performance, failure\n26\tmodes exploring the explore v exploit lens for\n27\tPathfinding conversations and voice agent reliability over all six worlds.\n28\t\n29\t1\n30\t\n31\tIntroduction\n32\t\n33\tSpeech-to-speech (S2S) voice agents capable of simultaneously understanding and generating audio\n34\tincreasingly exhibit remarkable agentic capabilities. Voice offers an expressive and convenient\n35\tinterface, making such agents preferable to textbased agents. In enterprise they answer customercare lines and help with re-booking flights, while\n36\tWork done during an internship at Centific.\n37\tEqual contribution. † Equal advising.\n38\t\n39\t∗\n40\t\n41\tFigure 1: Reliability curves for voice-agent performance\n42\tover all six worlds. Dashed: Pass@k, at least one pass\n43\tin k attempts; solid: Passk , all k. Voice Think Fast\n44\tleads both.\n45\t\n46\tfor consumers they act as a constant companion,\n47\thelping them find their way around a new city or\n48\trun daily errands.\n49\tEach of these is a conversation with nuanced\n50\tconversational dynamics and analytical demands:\n51\treconciling records, guiding users through busy\n52\tstreets, and doing both while contending with muffled speech, background noise and an emotionally\n53\tdiverse range of users.\n54\tFull-duplex voice agent research has proliferated\n55\tacross academia and industry. Benchmarks like τ Voice showed that voice agents retain only 30–45%\n56\tof text-agent capability on identical grounded tasks\n57\tunder realistic audio (Ray et al., 2026), and EVABench showed that no system is simultaneously\n58\tgood at task accuracy and conversational experience (Bogavelli et al., 2026). Full-Duplex-Bench\n59\tprogressed from static turn-taking probes to real\n60\tdisfluent speech with chained tool calls (Lin et al.,\n61\t2025b, 2026b, 2025a, 2026a), while parallel lines\n62\tisolate interruption and repair (Modi et al., 2026;\n63\tSalimi et al., 2026; Lu et al., 2026).\n64\tWhile the difficulty and framing of these tasks\n65\t\n66\t\f(a)\n67\t\n68\t(b)\n69\t\n70\tFigure 2: D UPLEX W ORLD at a glance. (a) The six worlds of an ordinary day, the authored scenarios (one wedge\n71\tper world), and conversation-time densities with hours of speech per world. (b) Six of the eleven conversation types\n72\t(Section 3.2), each with a verbatim passing excerpt from the scored corpus; far right, the two Pathfinding-only types\n73\twith the walked route against the ideal routes. Failing analogues of all eight tiles are in Figure 6 (appendix).\n74\t\n75\tand evaluations were justified at the time, the voice\n76\tagents of today are far more capable and deserve\n77\tbenchmarks that keep up with their rapid development. While prior benchmarks pursued coverage\n78\tacross domains in their own ways, they failed to\n79\tquestion the base premise on which voice agents\n80\tare built: their ability to integrate seamlessly into\n81\tdaily life.\n82\tTo address these issues, we introduce D UPLEX W ORLD, a benchmark for unified, holistic evaluation of voice agents across conversational and\n83\tanalytical tasks emerging from six diverse worlds\n84\tand eleven conversation types. The banking, insurance, travel, healthcare and logistics worlds consolidate and enhance evaluation along domains similar\n85\tto those explored in existing benchmarks, while\n86\tPathfinding introduces a new frontier for testing\n87\tvoice-agentic capability. Our contributions are:\n88\t• Six worlds spanning 156 scenarios and eleven\n89\tconversation types, expanding the gamut of\n90\tvoice-agentic evaluation. The first navigation\n91\tworld for full-duplex voice agents joins five enterprise worlds, with nine analytical interaction\n92\tshapes shared across the enterprise worlds and\n93\ttwo that only navigation elicits: the broadest\n94\tcoverage of any voice-agent benchmark (156\n95\tauthored scenarios, 3,825 scored conversations;\n96\tSections 3.1–3.2).\n97\t\n98\t• One unified evaluation suite across all of it.\n99\tTwelve metrics in three pillars run under one\n100\tharness and one configuration in every world: to\n101\tour knowledge the first single suite spanning this\n102\tbreadth of domains and demands (Section 4.3).\n103\t• Evidence that the three capabilities do not\n104\ttravel together. Pass@1 for one system spans\n105\t0.200–0.674 across worlds differing only in subject matter while the ranking barely moves; the\n106\tbest conversationalists are not the best taskcompleters; acoustic quality does not predict\n107\tcompetence; the under-effort share π − predicts\n108\tsuccess at ρ = −0.85; and read through an\n109\texplore–exploit lens, the heaviest explorers in\n110\tPathfinding arrive least (Section 5).\n111\t\n112\t2\n113\t\n114\tRelated Work\n115\t\n116\tTask-oriented agent benchmarks with verifiable outcomes. τ -bench introduced databasestate grading and Passk , the probability that all\n117\tk runs of a scenario succeed, for text agents in retail and airline customer service (Yao et al., 2024),\n118\tand τ 2 -bench added a telecom domain in which\n119\tthe user also holds tools, reporting an 18–25 point\n120\tPass@1 drop when agents move from acting alone\n121\tto guiding a user (Barres et al., 2025). These benchmarks fixed the right question, does the world end\n122\tup in the right state, but asked it of text agents. D U -\n123\t\n124\t\ffull duplex\n125\tdynamics\n126\tagentic task\n127\tnaturalness\n128\treliability Passk\n129\tsim. valid.\n130\tharness sens.\n131\tnav. world\n132\tτ -bench (2024)\n133\tτ 2 -bench (2025)\n134\tFDB v1–v2 (2025b)\n135\tTalking Turns (2025)\n136\tHumDial (2026)\n137\tEchoChain (2026)\n138\tIHBench (2026)\n139\tFDB v3 (2026a)\n140\tτ -Voice (2026)\n141\tEVA-Bench (2026)\n142\t\n143\t− − ✓ − ✓ − − −\n144\t− − ✓ − ✓ − − −\n145\t✓ ✓ − − − − − −\n146\t✓ ✓ − − − − − −\n147\t✓ ✓ − ✓ − − − −\n148\t✓ ✓ ✓ − − − − −\n149\t✓ ✓ ✓ − − − − −\n150\t✓ ✓ ✓ − − − − −\n151\t✓ − ✓ − ✓ − − −\n152\t✓ ✓ ✓ − ✓ ✓ − −\n153\t\n154\tD UPLEX W ORLD (ours) ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓\n155\t\n156\tTable 1: Comparison of D UPLEX W ORLD with prior\n157\tvoice-agent and text-agent benchmarks. The three\n158\tgrouped columns are the metric pillars of Section 4.3.\n159\tReliability: a multi-run repeatability metric (Passk );\n160\tsim. valid.: automated user-simulator validation; harness sens.: quantified sensitivity to harness configuration; nav. world: a navigation world.\n161\t\n162\tPLEX W ORLD keeps their discipline (state grading,\n163\t\n164\tgold actions, Passk ) and carries it into full-duplex\n165\tvoice, across six worlds instead of two or three\n166\tdomains.\n167\tFull-duplex conversational dynamics. A second line asks whether a system can hold the floor:\n168\tFull-Duplex-Bench defined automatic turn-taking\n169\tmetrics and its successors added overlap handling\n170\tand a live automated examiner (Lin et al., 2025b,\n171\t2026b, 2025a); Talking Turns judges turn-taking\n172\tagainst human conversation (Arora et al., 2025);\n173\tthe ICASSP 2026 HumDial challenge released\n174\tdual-channel real human dialogues (Zhao et al.,\n175\t2026; Wang et al., 2026); and a recent survey organises the architectural space by where the duplex\n176\tdecision sits in the model stack (Lu et al., 2026).\n177\tThese were the right first tests, and the current generation of commercial realtime systems has largely\n178\tgrown past them; our results sharpen the point by\n179\tshowing that dynamics metrics alone are winnable\n180\tby silence (Section 5). FDB-v3, Talking Turns and\n181\tHumDial evaluate on real human audio, and we\n182\tdo not (Limitations). D UPLEX W ORLD therefore\n183\tkeeps conversational dynamics as one pillar of a\n184\tunified suite, paired with capability metrics that\n185\tsilence cannot win.\n186\tGrounded voice-agent benchmarks. The\n187\tnewest line puts stronger voice agents on verifiable\n188\ttasks: τ -Voice with a tick-based orchestrator over\n189\t278 grounded tasks (Ray et al., 2026), EVA-Bench\n190\t\n191\twith validation-gated simulation over 213 enterprise scenarios (Bogavelli et al., 2026), FDB-v3\n192\twith real disfluent speech and chained tool calls\n193\t(Lin et al., 2026a), and the interruption-recovery\n194\tsuites EchoChain and IHBench (Modi et al., 2026;\n195\tSalimi et al., 2026). Harder tests for better agents,\n196\tyet in every one of them the agent actuates, a record\n197\tsystem adjudicates, and nothing happens between\n198\tutterances, and the interruption work varies only\n199\tthe user’s intent (Table 1). D UPLEX W ORLD spans\n200\tthat setting and a navigation world under one\n201\tharness and one metric suite, and it is hard even\n202\tfor this new generation: no system in our corpus\n203\texceeds Pass@1 of 0.674 in any single world, or\n204\t0.533 in the world that moves on its own clock.\n205\t\n206\t3\n207\t\n208\tThe D UPLEX W ORLD Benchmark\n209\t\n210\tD UPLEX W ORLD pairs a voice agent with a simulated user in six worlds of an ordinary day for 156\n211\tauthored scenarios, of which 144 carry the numbers in this paper. Every world is conversational\n212\tand every world is analytical: each couples live\n213\tfull-duplex speech to a verifiable task whose correctness the transcript alone cannot decide. Every\n214\tworld is scored on one twelve-metric suite spanning three pillars (conversational dynamics, agentic\n215\tcapability, naturalness), and no composite is ever\n216\tformed (one word per level of the hierarchy; Appendix A). Every scenario was authored by us over\n217\tmultiple rounds of experimentation, including trials\n218\twith text-mode language models; the task-design\n219\tdiscipline follows τ -bench and EVA-Bench (Yao\n220\tet al., 2024; Bogavelli et al., 2026).\n221\t3.1\n222\t\n223\tTask formulation\n224\t\n225\tAn episode pairs one voice agent with one simulated user in one world for a bounded number of\n226\tticks. The world exposes a state s, a tool interface and a graded end condition. The agent perceives only audio and tool returns, and it emits\n227\tonly audio and tool calls. When the episode\n228\tends, the terminal state is scored against a gold\n229\tstate s⋆ authored with the scenario, so success is\n230\ta property of the world rather than of the transcript. State advances through whichever effectors the world grants, st+1 = T (st , at , ut ). In\n231\tBanking, Insurance, Travel, Healthcare and\n232\tLogistics the records move only through the\n233\tagent’s tool calls at . In Pathfinding the world\n234\tmoves only through the walker’s physical actions\n235\tut , which the agent can influence through language\n236\t\n237\t\falone, so state there can change while both parties\n238\tare silent, and an utterance planned at tick t can\n239\tbe wrong by the time it lands at tick t + k. The\n240\tfive enterprise worlds share one premise. A caller\n241\treaches an institution’s telephone line after selfservice has already refused them. The agent owns\n242\tthe call and may not route away. The governing\n243\tclause corpus is withheld from the system prompt,\n244\tso knowing when to look something up is itself\n245\tunder test. The agent cannot see the caller’s screen\n246\tor surroundings, and credentials travel by voice\n247\tover a telephone codec, a deliberate stressor. What\n248\tdistinguishes the five worlds is what decides correctness (Table 7), and several regimes are enforced\n249\tin the tool surface itself, which makes the unsafe\n250\tbehaviour unrepresentable rather than discouraged\n251\t(Appendix D). Pathfinding asks the same question,\n252\tdoes the world end up in the right state, but its\n253\tcorrectness is perceptual rather than institutional.\n254\tA voice copilot must walk a pedestrian, one street\n255\tcorner at a time, to a named entrance on a synthetic\n256\t8 × 8 urban grid whose two superblocks defeat\n257\tnaive rectilinear reasoning. The premise is an information asymmetry. The walker holds their real\n258\tjunction and heading but knows nothing of the grid;\n259\tthe copilot holds the complete map and approximate GPS, but no tool returns the walker’s facing,\n260\tand directions() returns absolute bearings the\n261\twalker cannot act on. Translating those bearings\n262\tinto “keep going straight” or “turn around”, against\n263\tthe copilot’s own inferred belief about the walker’s\n264\tfacing, is the operative skill, and it mirrors how\n265\tproduction routing APIs behave. The walker must\n266\tbe standing at the destination when the call ends,\n267\tnot merely have passed through it; route efficiency\n268\tenters the reward and the acoustic channel is varied. The full tool surface, belief probe and arrival\n269\tscoring are in Appendix E, and Appendix F draws\n270\ta solved and an unsolved run of each Pathfinding\n271\ttype on the map itself.\n272\t3.2\n273\t\n274\tsystem\n275\t\n276\tAPI identifier\n277\t\n278\tNova 2 Sonic\n279\tGemini-3.1-Flash-Live\n280\tGPT-Realtime-2.1\n281\tGPT-Realtime-2.1-mini\n282\tGrok Voice Think Fast 1.0\n283\t\n284\tamazon.nova-2-sonic-v1:0\n285\tgemini-3.1-flash-live-preview\n286\tgpt-realtime-2.1\n287\tgpt-realtime-2.1-mini\n288\txai-realtime\n289\t\n290\tTable 2: Systems evaluated, as recorded in the run logs.\n291\tAll are speech-to-speech; elsewhere we write each system as its vendor mark plus the version alone ( 2\n292\tSonic, 3.1-Flash-Live, Realtime-2.1, Realtime2.1-mini, Voice Think Fast). Access windows are in\n293\tAppendix C.\n294\t\n295\tone detour survives, and ALL - DAY ASSISTANCE,\n296\twhere the walker reveals a second destination only\n297\ton arriving at the first. Each of the three types has\n298\tthree authored scenarios, giving nine campaigned\n299\tPathfinding scenarios. Every scenario is run five\n300\ttimes per channel over two acoustic channels, so\n301\tPathfinding contributes 9 × 5 × 2 = 90 conversations per system; the realistic channel’s 45 carry\n302\tits headline cells (Section 4.2). Together with the\n303\t135 enterprise scenarios, these nine complete the\n304\t144 scenarios that carry every number in this paper;\n305\tthe remaining twelve of the 156 authored scenarios\n306\tbelong to four Pathfinding variants that are released\n307\tbut not run (Appendix E). Types are shapes, not levels: they are unordered, and parameters that govern\n308\tdifficulty (escalation count, candidate-record count,\n309\tclosure count) live inside a type. Figure 2 shows a\n310\tverbatim passing excerpt for six of the types; Figure 6 (appendix) shows each type’s characteristic\n311\tfailure from the same corpus.\n312\tAll six worlds share one volatility stressor: the\n313\tworld can change with no notification, discoverably only by re-querying. The three sources of\n314\tbelief–world divergence this exposes, and the different repairs each demands, are catalogued in Appendix D. A benchmark’s honest description also\n315\tincludes what its reward cannot see; ours is audited\n316\tin Appendix M.\n317\t\n318\tTask design\n319\t\n320\tEvery scenario instantiates one of eleven conversation types (Table 8): a domain-independent interaction shape, filled in by the world’s premise,\n321\trecords, rules and risk tiers. The five enterprise\n322\tworlds each instantiate types 1–9, three scenarios\n323\tper type: 27 per world, 135 in total. Pathfinding\n324\tinstantiates SINGLE INTENT (its base routes) together with the two types that only navigation elicits: REROUTING, where closures absent from the\n325\tcopilot’s map seal every route it can see and exactly\n326\t\n327\t4\n328\t\n329\tExperimental Setup\n330\t\n331\t4.1\n332\t\n333\tSystems\n334\t\n335\tTable 2 lists the five commercial realtime speechto-speech systems evaluated, identified exactly as\n336\tthe run records identify them. All five are served\n337\tover WebSocket realtime endpoints through one\n338\tadapter at provider defaults; full serving details are\n339\tin Appendix C.\n340\t\n341\t\f4.2\n342\t\n343\tEvaluation strategy\n344\t\n345\tHarness. Our harness is built by taking inspiration from τ -Voice and EVA-Bench (Ray et al.,\n346\t2026; Bogavelli et al., 2026): it follows their tickbased design and simulator instrumentation, and\n347\tAppendix B states precisely what is inherited and\n348\twhat we added. Simulated time advances in 200 ms\n349\tticks; model latency never shifts an event’s position, so slow and fast systems produce comparable\n350\ttraces, and tool results deliver on the next tick so\n351\taudio never stalls on tool latency. Holding one harness fixed across all six worlds is deliberate: uniformity on the harness side is what lets the eleven\n352\tconversation types ask novel questions along new\n353\taxes, and it is what makes the unified evaluation of\n354\tSection 4.3 possible. Every run pairs the agent under test with a user simulator (gpt-5.6-luna) that\n355\twrites the caller’s or walker’s words; on the realistic\n356\tchannel a decision model (claude-haiku-4.5) additionally answers “should I interrupt” and “should\n357\tI backchannel” at a fixed cadence while the agent\n358\tspeaks, and the clean channel disables interruptions\n359\tand backchannels, so no decision model participates (Table 6). Turn-taking control sits entirely\n360\ton the user side, and the agent is never told any\n361\tthreshold. Our own additions, and how little of\n362\tthe type taxonomy depends on them, are in Appendix B. Infrastructure failure means rerun and\n363\tnever score; agent non-response is a valid scored\n364\tend state; simulator goal adherence is measured,\n365\tand never gates reward (Bogavelli et al., 2026).\n366\tTask mechanics. Tools in the enterprise worlds\n367\tare declarative mocks over a seeded record store;\n368\teach scenario names a gold action list and, where\n369\twhat is said or refused is the point, a naturallanguage assertion. In Pathfinding, walker motion\n370\tis maneuver-driven and the copilot’s reads return\n371\tnatural-language strings; the full tool surface is in\n372\tAppendix E.\n373\tConditions and runs. One matched configuration across all six worlds: speech complexity\n374\tregular (distractors at 0.7/min), step cap 1200 s,\n375\twall-clock timeout 1200 s, server VAD threshold\n376\t0.2, fixed seed, persona pinned per scenario. Both\n377\tchannels are G.711 µ-law 8 kHz telephony; clean\n378\tadds no degradation, and realistic adds recorded\n379\tnoise, bursts, frame drops, muffling and speech\n380\tinserts (Appendix G). The enterprise worlds run\n381\trealistic-only; Pathfinding runs clean and realistic, and its headline cells in Table 3 use the re-\n382\t\n383\talistic channel, the same basis as the enterprise\n384\tworlds, with both channels decomposed in Table 14.\n385\tFive runs per scenario per system per channel, uniform: 135 conversations per system in each enterprise world, 90 in Pathfinding, 3,825 in total (Appendix P). Uniform n = 5 is what makes Pass3\n386\twell defined.\n387\tStatistics. Every cell in Table 3 carries a 95%\n388\tpercentile-bootstrap interval (Pass3 resampled over\n389\tscenarios). Overlapping intervals mean a difference\n390\tis not resolved, and we do not report unresolved\n391\tdifferences as rankings. Because the five enterprise worlds share harness, metrics, configuration\n392\tand type taxonomy, the interval a system’s score\n393\tspans across them is a measured null, how far a\n394\tvoice-agent number moves when only subject matter changes, and Pathfinding is read against that\n395\treference throughout Section 5.\n396\t4.3\n397\t\n398\tMetrics\n399\t\n400\tTwelve metrics in three pillars, no composite; Table 11 fixes every name, symbol and range, and\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "5ce68dc9-4736-49eb-a471-b64b3754ad46", "uuid": "1e094f2b-0724-4d34-95f0-8789e10104d5", "timestamp": "2026-08-12T08:50:46.851Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/duplexworld.txt", "content": "D UPLEX W ORLD: Can voice agents help you get through the day?\nAryan Vijay Bhosale1,2∗ , Harshit Rajgarhia1∗ , Akhil Pothanapalli1∗ ,\nAsif Shaik1 , Abhishek Mukherji1† , Dinesh Manocha2†\n1\nCentific Global Solutions Inc. 2 University of Maryland\n{aryan.bhosale, harshit.rajgarhia}@centific.com\nhttps://duplexworld.github.io\n\narXiv:2608.10716v1 [cs.SD] 11 Aug 2026\n\nAbstract\nSpeech-to-speech (S2S) voice agents are increasingly being incorporated into enterprise\nfor customer care and as daily companions for\nconsumers owing to the ease of the conversational modality over text. However, existing\nbenchmarks fail to holistically evaluate voice\nagents along axes that really matter and are\nshaped as tests of agentic tool calling against\na database. We believe they fail to adequately\naccount for the diversity of conversational dialogue that mundane activities introduce and further, never test how faithfully an agent can assist on tasks that move beyond database manipulation. To tackle this D UPLEX W ORLD introduces six worlds where voice agents are especially useful: banking, insurance, travel, healthcare and logistics, and Pathfinding. Agents are\nevaluated on eleven different types of conversations across 156 scenarios (350+ hours of conversation), each testing conversational and analytical capability to varying degrees. Through\nextensive evaluation comprising agentic, conversational and speech-naturalness metrics, we\nshow that even the best voice agents leave substantial room for improvement on all 3 axes\n(Pass@1: 0.490, turn-taking: 0.653, DNSMOS:\n3.378). We perform extensive analysis on agentic v conversational performance, world- and\nconversation type-wise performance, failure\nmodes exploring the explore v exploit lens for\nPathfinding conversations and voice agent reliability over all six worlds.\n\n1\n\nIntroduction\n\nSpeech-to-speech (S2S) voice agents capable of simultaneously understanding and generating audio\nincreasingly exhibit remarkable agentic capabilities. Voice offers an expressive and convenient\ninterface, making such agents preferable to textbased agents. In enterprise they answer customercare lines and help with re-booking flights, while\nWork done during an internship at Centific.\nEqual contribution. † Equal advising.\n\n∗\n\nFigure 1: Reliability curves for voice-agent performance\nover all six worlds. Dashed: Pass@k, at least one pass\nin k attempts; solid: Passk , all k. Voice Think Fast\nleads both.\n\nfor consumers they act as a constant companion,\nhelping them find their way around a new city or\nrun daily errands.\nEach of these is a conversation with nuanced\nconversational dynamics and analytical demands:\nreconciling records, guiding users through busy\nstreets, and doing both while contending with muffled speech, background noise and an emotionally\ndiverse range of users.\nFull-duplex voice agent research has proliferated\nacross academia and industry. Benchmarks like τ Voice showed that voice agents retain only 30–45%\nof text-agent capability on identical grounded tasks\nunder realistic audio (Ray et al., 2026), and EVABench showed that no system is simultaneously\ngood at task accuracy and conversational experience (Bogavelli et al., 2026). Full-Duplex-Bench\nprogressed from static turn-taking probes to real\ndisfluent speech with chained tool calls (Lin et al.,\n2025b, 2026b, 2025a, 2026a), while parallel lines\nisolate interruption and repair (Modi et al., 2026;\nSalimi et al., 2026; Lu et al., 2026).\nWhile the difficulty and framing of these tasks\n\n\f(a)\n\n(b)\n\nFigure 2: D UPLEX W ORLD at a glance. (a) The six worlds of an ordinary day, the authored scenarios (one wedge\nper world), and conversation-time densities with hours of speech per world. (b) Six of the eleven conversation types\n(Section 3.2), each with a verbatim passing excerpt from the scored corpus; far right, the two Pathfinding-only types\nwith the walked route against the ideal routes. Failing analogues of all eight tiles are in Figure 6 (appendix).\n\nand evaluations were justified at the time, the voice\nagents of today are far more capable and deserve\nbenchmarks that keep up with their rapid development. While prior benchmarks pursued coverage\nacross domains in their own ways, they failed to\nquestion the base premise on which voice agents\nare built: their ability to integrate seamlessly into\ndaily life.\nTo address these issues, we introduce D UPLEX W ORLD, a benchmark for unified, holistic evaluation of voice agents across conversational and\nanalytical tasks emerging from six diverse worlds\nand eleven conversation types. The banking, insurance, travel, healthcare and logistics worlds consolidate and enhance evaluation along domains similar\nto those explored in existing benchmarks, while\nPathfinding introduces a new frontier for testing\nvoice-agentic capability. Our contributions are:\n• Six worlds spanning 156 scenarios and eleven\nconversation types, expanding the gamut of\nvoice-agentic evaluation. The first navigation\nworld for full-duplex voice agents joins five enterprise worlds, with nine analytical interaction\nshapes shared across the enterprise worlds and\ntwo that only navigation elicits: the broadest\ncoverage of any voice-agent benchmark (156\nauthored scenarios, 3,825 scored conversations;\nSections 3.1–3.2).\n\n• One unified evaluation suite across all of it.\nTwelve metrics in three pillars run under one\nharness and one configuration in every world: to\nour knowledge the first single suite spanning this\nbreadth of domains and demands (Section 4.3).\n• Evidence that the three capabilities do not\ntravel together. Pass@1 for one system spans\n0.200–0.674 across worlds differing only in subject matter while the ranking barely moves; the\nbest conversationalists are not the best taskcompleters; acoustic quality does not predict\ncompetence; the under-effort share π − predicts\nsuccess at ρ = −0.85; and read through an\nexplore–exploit lens, the heaviest explorers in\nPathfinding arrive least (Section 5).\n\n2\n\nRelated Work\n\nTask-oriented agent benchmarks with verifiable outcomes. τ -bench introduced databasestate grading and Passk , the probability that all\nk runs of a scenario succeed, for text agents in retail and airline customer service (Yao et al., 2024),\nand τ 2 -bench added a telecom domain in which\nthe user also holds tools, reporting an 18–25 point\nPass@1 drop when agents move from acting alone\nto guiding a user (Barres et al., 2025). These benchmarks fixed the right question, does the world end\nup in the right state, but asked it of text agents. D U -\n\n\ffull duplex\ndynamics\nagentic task\nnaturalness\nreliability Passk\nsim. valid.\nharness sens.\nnav. world\nτ -bench (2024)\nτ 2 -bench (2025)\nFDB v1–v2 (2025b)\nTalking Turns (2025)\nHumDial (2026)\nEchoChain (2026)\nIHBench (2026)\nFDB v3 (2026a)\nτ -Voice (2026)\nEVA-Bench (2026)\n\n− − ✓ − ✓ − − −\n− − ✓ − ✓ − − −\n✓ ✓ − − − − − −\n✓ ✓ − − − − − −\n✓ ✓ − ✓ − − − −\n✓ ✓ ✓ − − − − −\n✓ ✓ ✓ − − − − −\n✓ ✓ ✓ − − − − −\n✓ − ✓ − ✓ − − −\n✓ ✓ ✓ − ✓ ✓ − −\n\nD UPLEX W ORLD (ours) ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓\n\nTable 1: Comparison of D UPLEX W ORLD with prior\nvoice-agent and text-agent benchmarks. The three\ngrouped columns are the metric pillars of Section 4.3.\nReliability: a multi-run repeatability metric (Passk );\nsim. valid.: automated user-simulator validation; harness sens.: quantified sensitivity to harness configuration; nav. world: a navigation world.\n\nPLEX W ORLD keeps their discipline (state grading,\n\ngold actions, Passk ) and carries it into full-duplex\nvoice, across six worlds instead of two or three\ndomains.\nFull-duplex conversational dynamics. A second line asks whether a system can hold the floor:\nFull-Duplex-Bench defined automatic turn-taking\nmetrics and its successors added overlap handling\nand a live automated examiner (Lin et al., 2025b,\n2026b, 2025a); Talking Turns judges turn-taking\nagainst human conversation (Arora et al., 2025);\nthe ICASSP 2026 HumDial challenge released\ndual-channel real human dialogues (Zhao et al.,\n2026; Wang et al., 2026); and a recent survey organises the architectural space by where the duplex\ndecision sits in the model stack (Lu et al., 2026).\nThese were the right first tests, and the current generation of commercial realtime systems has largely\ngrown past them; our results sharpen the point by\nshowing that dynamics metrics alone are winnable\nby silence (Section 5). FDB-v3, Talking Turns and\nHumDial evaluate on real human audio, and we\ndo not (Limitations). D UPLEX W ORLD therefore\nkeeps conversational dynamics as one pillar of a\nunified suite, paired with capability metrics that\nsilence cannot win.\nGrounded voice-agent benchmarks. The\nnewest line puts stronger voice agents on verifiable\ntasks: τ -Voice with a tick-based orchestrator over\n278 grounded tasks (Ray et al., 2026), EVA-Bench\n\nwith validation-gated simulation over 213 enterprise scenarios (Bogavelli et al., 2026), FDB-v3\nwith real disfluent speech and chained tool calls\n(Lin et al., 2026a), and the interruption-recovery\nsuites EchoChain and IHBench (Modi et al., 2026;\nSalimi et al., 2026). Harder tests for better agents,\nyet in every one of them the agent actuates, a record\nsystem adjudicates, and nothing happens between\nutterances, and the interruption work varies only\nthe user’s intent (Table 1). D UPLEX W ORLD spans\nthat setting and a navigation world under one\nharness and one metric suite, and it is hard even\nfor this new generation: no system in our corpus\nexceeds Pass@1 of 0.674 in any single world, or\n0.533 in the world that moves on its own clock.\n\n3\n\nThe D UPLEX W ORLD Benchmark\n\nD UPLEX W ORLD pairs a voice agent with a simulated user in six worlds of an ordinary day for 156\nauthored scenarios, of which 144 carry the numbers in this paper. Every world is conversational\nand every world is analytical: each couples live\nfull-duplex speech to a verifiable task whose correctness the transcript alone cannot decide. Every\nworld is scored on one twelve-metric suite spanning three pillars (conversational dynamics, agentic\ncapability, naturalness), and no composite is ever\nformed (one word per level of the hierarchy; Appendix A). Every scenario was authored by us over\nmultiple rounds of experimentation, including trials\nwith text-mode language models; the task-design\ndiscipline follows τ -bench and EVA-Bench (Yao\net al., 2024; Bogavelli et al., 2026).\n3.1\n\nTask formulation\n\nAn episode pairs one voice agent with one simulated user in one world for a bounded number of\nticks. The world exposes a state s, a tool interface and a graded end condition. The agent perceives only audio and tool returns, and it emits\nonly audio and tool calls. When the episode\nends, the terminal state is scored against a gold\nstate s⋆ authored with the scenario, so success is\na property of the world rather than of the transcript. State advances through whichever effectors the world grants, st+1 = T (st , at , ut ). In\nBanking, Insurance, Travel, Healthcare and\nLogistics the records move only through the\nagent’s tool calls at . In Pathfinding the world\nmoves only through the walker’s physical actions\nut , which the agent can influence through language\n\n\falone, so state there can change while both parties\nare silent, and an utterance planned at tick t can\nbe wrong by the time it lands at tick t + k. The\nfive enterprise worlds share one premise. A caller\nreaches an institution’s telephone line after selfservice has already refused them. The agent owns\nthe call and may not route away. The governing\nclause corpus is withheld from the system prompt,\nso knowing when to look something up is itself\nunder test. The agent cannot see the caller’s screen\nor surroundings, and credentials travel by voice\nover a telephone codec, a deliberate stressor. What\ndistinguishes the five worlds is what decides correctness (Table 7), and several regimes are enforced\nin the tool surface itself, which makes the unsafe\nbehaviour unrepresentable rather than discouraged\n(Appendix D). Pathfinding asks the same question,\ndoes the world end up in the right state, but its\ncorrectness is perceptual rather than institutional.\nA voice copilot must walk a pedestrian, one street\ncorner at a time, to a named entrance on a synthetic\n8 × 8 urban grid whose two superblocks defeat\nnaive rectilinear reasoning. The premise is an information asymmetry. The walker holds their real\njunction and heading but knows nothing of the grid;\nthe copilot holds the complete map and approximate GPS, but no tool returns the walker’s facing,\nand directions() returns absolute bearings the\nwalker cannot act on. Translating those bearings\ninto “keep going straight” or “turn around”, against\nthe copilot’s own inferred belief about the walker’s\nfacing, is the operative skill, and it mirrors how\nproduction routing APIs behave. The walker must\nbe standing at the destination when the call ends,\nnot merely have passed through it; route efficiency\nenters the reward and the acoustic channel is varied. The full tool surface, belief probe and arrival\nscoring are in Appendix E, and Appendix F draws\na solved and an unsolved run of each Pathfinding\ntype on the map itself.\n3.2\n\nsystem\n\nAPI identifier\n\nNova 2 Sonic\nGemini-3.1-Flash-Live\nGPT-Realtime-2.1\nGPT-Realtime-2.1-mini\nGrok Voice Think Fast 1.0\n\namazon.nova-2-sonic-v1:0\ngemini-3.1-flash-live-preview\ngpt-realtime-2.1\ngpt-realtime-2.1-mini\nxai-realtime\n\nTable 2: Systems evaluated, as recorded in the run logs.\nAll are speech-to-speech; elsewhere we write each system as its vendor mark plus the version alone ( 2\nSonic, 3.1-Flash-Live, Realtime-2.1, Realtime2.1-mini, Voice Think Fast). Access windows are in\nAppendix C.\n\none detour survives, and ALL - DAY ASSISTANCE,\nwhere the walker reveals a second destination only\non arriving at the first. Each of the three types has\nthree authored scenarios, giving nine campaigned\nPathfinding scenarios. Every scenario is run five\ntimes per channel over two acoustic channels, so\nPathfinding contributes 9 × 5 × 2 = 90 conversations per system; the realistic channel’s 45 carry\nits headline cells (Section 4.2). Together with the\n135 enterprise scenarios, these nine complete the\n144 scenarios that carry every number in this paper;\nthe remaining twelve of the 156 authored scenarios\nbelong to four Pathfinding variants that are released\nbut not run (Appendix E). Types are shapes, not levels: they are unordered, and parameters that govern\ndifficulty (escalation count, candidate-record count,\nclosure count) live inside a type. Figure 2 shows a\nverbatim passing excerpt for six of the types; Figure 6 (appendix) shows each type’s characteristic\nfailure from the same corpus.\nAll six worlds share one volatility stressor: the\nworld can change with no notification, discoverably only by re-querying. The three sources of\nbelief–world divergence this exposes, and the different repairs each demands, are catalogued in Appendix D. A benchmark’s honest description also\nincludes what its reward cannot see; ours is audited\nin Appendix M.\n\nTask design\n\nEvery scenario instantiates one of eleven conversation types (Table 8): a domain-independent interaction shape, filled in by the world’s premise,\nrecords, rules and risk tiers. The five enterprise\nworlds each instantiate types 1–9, three scenarios\nper type: 27 per world, 135 in total. Pathfinding\ninstantiates SINGLE INTENT (its base routes) together with the two types that only navigation elicits: REROUTING, where closures absent from the\ncopilot’s map seal every route it can see and exactly\n\n4\n\nExperimental Setup\n\n4.1\n\nSystems\n\nTable 2 lists the five commercial realtime speechto-speech systems evaluated, identified exactly as\nthe run records identify them. All five are served\nover WebSocket realtime endpoints through one\nadapter at provider defaults; full serving details are\nin Appendix C.\n\n\f4.2\n\nEvaluation strategy\n\nHarness. Our harness is built by taking inspiration from τ -Voice and EVA-Bench (Ray et al.,\n2026; Bogavelli et al., 2026): it follows their tickbased design and simulator instrumentation, and\nAppendix B states precisely what is inherited and\nwhat we added. Simulated time advances in 200 ms\nticks; model latency never shifts an event’s position, so slow and fast systems produce comparable\ntraces, and tool results deliver on the next tick so\naudio never stalls on tool latency. Holding one harness fixed across all six worlds is deliberate: uniformity on the harness side is what lets the eleven\nconversation types ask novel questions along new\naxes, and it is what makes the unified evaluation of\nSection 4.3 possible. Every run pairs the agent under test with a user simulator (gpt-5.6-luna) that\nwrites the caller’s or walker’s words; on the realistic\nchannel a decision model (claude-haiku-4.5) additionally answers “should I interrupt” and “should\nI backchannel” at a fixed cadence while the agent\nspeaks, and the clean channel disables interruptions\nand backchannels, so no decision model participates (Table 6). Turn-taking control sits entirely\non the user side, and the agent is never told any\nthreshold. Our own additions, and how little of\nthe type taxonomy depends on them, are in Appendix B. Infrastructure failure means rerun and\nnever score; agent non-response is a valid scored\nend state; simulator goal adherence is measured,\nand never gates reward (Bogavelli et al., 2026).\nTask mechanics. Tools in the enterprise worlds\nare declarative mocks over a seeded record store;\neach scenario names a gold action list and, where\nwhat is said or refused is the point, a naturallanguage assertion. In Pathfinding, walker motion\nis maneuver-driven and the copilot’s reads return\nnatural-language strings; the full tool surface is in\nAppendix E.\nConditions and runs. One matched configuration across all six worlds: speech complexity\nregular (distractors at 0.7/min), step cap 1200 s,\nwall-clock timeout 1200 s, server VAD threshold\n0.2, fixed seed, persona pinned per scenario. Both\nchannels are G.711 µ-law 8 kHz telephony; clean\nadds no degradation, and realistic adds recorded\nnoise, bursts, frame drops, muffling and speech\ninserts (Appendix G). The enterprise worlds run\nrealistic-only; Pathfinding runs clean and realistic, and its headline cells in Table 3 use the re-\n\nalistic channel, the same basis as the enterprise\nworlds, with both channels decomposed in Table 14.\nFive runs per scenario per system per channel, uniform: 135 conversations per system in each enterprise world, 90 in Pathfinding, 3,825 in total (Appendix P). Uniform n = 5 is what makes Pass3\nwell defined.\nStatistics. Every cell in Table 3 carries a 95%\npercentile-bootstrap interval (Pass3 resampled over\nscenarios). Overlapping intervals mean a difference\nis not resolved, and we do not report unresolved\ndifferences as rankings. Because the five enterprise worlds share harness, metrics, configuration\nand type taxonomy, the interval a system’s score\nspans across them is a measured null, how far a\nvoice-agent number moves when only subject matter changes, and Pathfinding is read against that\nreference throughout Section 5.\n4.3\n\nMetrics\n\nTwelve metrics in three pillars, no composite; Table 11 fixes every name, symbol and range, and", "numLines": 400, "startLine": 1, "totalLines": 3094}}}
