{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-bf472e7860ef60a6", "type": "tool_result", "content": "900\t2S\n901\t\n902\tall\n903\tsix\n904\t\n905\tConversational Dynamics\n906\t\n907\tPass@1\n908\t\n909\tGS\n910\t\n911\t3.1\n912\t\n913\tCP\n914\t\n915\tSEL\n916\t\n917\tFAI\n918\t\n919\tTable 3: Main results across the six worlds (realistic channel; n = 135 conversations per enterprise cell, 45 per\n920\tPathfinding cell). Worlds by mark ( Banking, Logistics, Healthcare, Insurance, Travel, Pathfinding);\n921\tsystems by vendor logo and version. Subscripts are 95% bootstrap half-widths. Bold marks the best system per\n922\tworld and column. GS is the goal-state check; Pass@1, Pass@3 and Pass3 are the single-run reward, at least one\n923\tpass in three draws, and all three of three. CP and FAI are on 1–3, MOS predictors on 1–5; the all six block is the\n924\tunweighted mean over worlds. Effort metrics are in Table 4.\n925\t\n926\t95% of its enterprise conversations. Pathfinding\n927\tinverts the pattern. There the strongest systems\n928\tover-work ( Gemini-3.1-Flash-Live exceeds its\n929\tbudget in 80% of conversations and still arrives\n930\tmost often) while the weak systems under-work,\n931\tbecause a copilot that has lost the walker stops issuing instructions. Across all worlds the under-effort\n932\tshare π − is the strongest process predictor of failure in the suite (Appendix K); it cannot be won by\n933\tsilence, because it is signed against the workload,\n934\tand we would recommend the field report it beside\n935\tevery completion score.\n936\t5.3\n937\t\n938\tWhat the auxiliary metrics reveal\n939\t\n940\tIn the enterprise worlds, the clearest auxiliary signal is the credential word error rate (Table 13, appendix). GPT-Realtime-2.1 hears best: 10.4%\n941\t\n942\terror on spoken credentials over the telephone\n943\tchannel, against 14.4 for Grok Voice Think Fast\n944\t1.0, 15.5 for Gemini-3.1-Flash-Live and 20.2 for\n945\tGPT-Realtime-2.1-mini, so distillation roughly\n946\tdoubles credential error inside one family; Nova\n947\t2 Sonic’s 33.3% is measured only on the minority of calls in which it attempted a credential at\n948\tall. Pooled, about one spoken credential in six\n949\tis mis-heard (17.1%), which puts a floor under\n950\tidentity verification for every system: recognition over a telephony channel, not reasoning, is\n951\tstill a first-order bottleneck. Pathfinding adds an\n952\tinstrument no enterprise world can: the exploration share, the fraction of walker moves made\n953\tunder uncertainty rather than on a confident instruction (Table 14, appendix). The world is, in\n954\teffect, an MDP, junctions for states and maneuvers\n955\t\n956\t\f2S\n957\t3.1\n958\t2.1\n959\tmini\n960\tVTF\n961\t2S\n962\t3.1\n963\t2.1\n964\tmini\n965\tVTF\n966\t2S\n967\t3.1\n968\t2.1\n969\tmini\n970\tVTF\n971\t\n972\tϱ+ (π + )\n973\t\n974\tϱ− (π − )\n975\t\n976\tϱ+ (π + )\n977\t\n978\tϱ− (π − )\n979\t\n980\t1.7782†\n981\t±.583\n982\t2.12661\n983\t±.200\n984\t1.80453\n985\t±.158\n986\t1.84053\n987\t±.149\n988\t1.84156\n989\t±.160\n990\t\n991\t0.11295\n992\t±.037\n993\t0.50729\n994\t±.072\n995\t0.31726\n996\t±.104\n997\t0.39341\n998\t±.078\n999\t0.49821\n1000\t±.128\n1001\t\n1002\t2.06127\n1003\t±.349\n1004\t1.79167\n1005\t±.176\n1006\t1.86083\n1007\t±.145\n1008\t2.22376\n1009\t±.203\n1010\t78\n1011\t1.895±.174\n1012\t\n1013\t0.14658\n1014\t±.057\n1015\t0.54717\n1016\t±.100\n1017\t0.3656†\n1018\t±.214\n1019\t0.4587†\n1020\t±.175\n1021\t4†\n1022\t0.375±.236\n1023\t\n1024\t1.60011†\n1025\t±.117\n1026\t1.79160\n1027\t±.147\n1028\t51\n1029\t1.728±.136\n1030\t50\n1031\t1.789±.150\n1032\t1.82852\n1033\t±.161\n1034\t\n1035\t0.18977\n1036\t±.050\n1037\t0.65510†\n1038\t±.077\n1039\t0.6447†\n1040\t±.119\n1041\t0.60316\n1042\t±.071\n1043\t0.6528†\n1044\t±.095\n1045\t\n1046\t2.32216\n1047\t±.439\n1048\t2.74878\n1049\t±.267\n1050\t2.61576\n1051\t±.220\n1052\t3.00271\n1053\t±.361\n1054\t2.76476\n1055\t±.262\n1056\t\n1057\t0.16164\n1058\t±.050\n1059\t0.4673†\n1060\t±.175\n1061\t0.3157†\n1062\t±.215\n1063\t0.57013†\n1064\t±.127\n1065\t4†\n1066\t0.583±.217\n1067\t\n1068\t1.90710†\n1069\t±.369\n1070\t2.27373\n1071\t±.217\n1072\t1.98573\n1073\t±.165\n1074\t2.28659\n1075\t±.266\n1076\t1.89967\n1077\t±.141\n1078\t\n1079\t0.21278\n1080\t±.053\n1081\t0.4119†\n1082\t±.172\n1083\t0.6265†\n1084\t±.106\n1085\t0.51119\n1086\t±.095\n1087\t0.4796†\n1088\t±.205\n1089\t\n1090\t4.25018†\n1091\t±3.716\n1092\t2.16980\n1093\t±.279\n1094\t1.47640†\n1095\t±.254\n1096\t1.52442†\n1097\t±.231\n1098\t47\n1099\t1.640±.165\n1100\t\n1101\t0.43973\n1102\t±.060\n1103\t0.59513†\n1104\t±.119\n1105\t0.63747\n1106\t±.058\n1107\t0.68151\n1108\t±.065\n1109\t44\n1110\t0.672±.077\n1111\t\n1112\t2S\n1113\t3.1\n1114\t2.1\n1115\tmini\n1116\tVTF\n1117\t2S\n1118\t3.1\n1119\t2.1\n1120\tmini\n1121\tVTF\n1122\t2S\n1123\t3.1\n1124\t2.1\n1125\tmini\n1126\tVTF\n1127\t\n1128\tTable 4: Effort against the task’s reference workload (ideal tool-call count in the enterprise worlds, optimal block count in Pathfinding): over-effort ϱ+ and\n1129\tunder-effort ϱ− ratios, conditioning shares π ± (%) as\n1130\tsuperscripts; † marks fewer than 20 conversations. Section 5.2 reads the table; 2S ’s ϱ+ = 4.25 rests on 8\n1131\tconversations.\n1132\t\n1133\tfor actions, so we read each system’s behaviour\n1134\tas a policy through the explore–exploit lens of\n1135\tthe agents literature. Systems probe with signreadings, look-arounds and trial walks, then commit, and the policies differ sharply by system and\n1136\tfar less by condition. Gemini-3.1-Flash-Live and\n1137\tGrok Voice Think Fast 1.0 explore on 16.2%\n1138\tand 22.7% of moves; GPT-Realtime-2.1-mini,\n1139\tGPT-Realtime-2.1 and Nova 2 Sonic on 40.3,\n1140\t43.4 and 51.2; realistic acoustics raise exploration\n1141\tfor most systems ( GPT-Realtime-2.1 from 41.1\n1142\tto 50.0 on SINGLE INTENT, Grok Voice Think\n1143\tFast 1.0 from 12.4 to 24.9 on REROUTING) without\n1144\treordering them, and exploration falls for most systems on ALL - DAY ASSISTANCE, where the second\n1145\tleg retraces known ground. Exploration is not the\n1146\tvirtue here that it is in reinforcement learning; in\n1147\tthis corpus the trade-off resolves entirely toward exploitation. In every condition and channel the two\n1148\tlowest explorers are the two most likely to arrive,\n1149\tthe highest explorer never arrives at all, and only\n1150\tsix non-arrivals in the whole realistic corpus end at\n1151\ta wrong destination; the rest run out of clock (Appendix E.1). Exploration in Pathfinding is mostly\n1152\trambling: motion spent recovering a belief about\n1153\tthe walker that the better systems never lost, and\n1154\tPass@1 beside the exploration column is what says\n1155\tthe wandering policies do not pay.\n1156\t\n1157\t6\n1158\t\n1159\tConclusion and Future Work\n1160\t\n1161\tWe introduced D UPLEX W ORLD, a benchmark that\n1162\tevaluates S2S voice agents across six worlds and\n1163\televen conversation types under one harness and\n1164\tone twelve-metric suite, and evaluated five commercial systems over 3,825 conversations. The\n1165\tresults are direct: even the best systems leave substantial room on every axis, with no system exceeding Pass@1 0.490, turn-taking 0.653 or DNSMOS\n1166\t3.378 pooled over the worlds (Figures 7 and 9).\n1167\tThe analysis yields four concrete findings. A\n1168\tscore depends on the world and the conversation\n1169\ttype as much as on the system: one system spans\n1170\tPass@1 0.200 to 0.674 across worlds that differ\n1171\tonly in subject matter. Conversational and agentic\n1172\tcapability are separate: the best conversationalists\n1173\tare not the best task-completers, and a system can\n1174\thold the floor gracefully while completing nothing (Figure 8). Acoustic quality tells you almost\n1175\tnothing about competence, while mis-heard credentials put a floor under identity verification for\n1176\tevery system. And in Pathfinding, read through an\n1177\texplore–exploit lens, the systems that explore most\n1178\tarrive least (Figure 10). The practical takeaway\n1179\tfor deployment is equally direct: report the undereffort share and the harness configuration beside\n1180\tevery score, because both move the numbers.\n1181\tFuture work includes a human-rating study to\n1182\tvalidate the judge-based metrics, campaigning the\n1183\treleased d_vague variant (Appendix E), a factorial\n1184\tstudy over the three harness dials (Appendix I), and\n1185\textending the benchmark to more languages and\n1186\treal recorded speech.\n1187\t\n1188\tLimitations\n1189\tPathfinding is a single world that raises several demands at once (who holds the effectors, what the\n1190\tagent can observe, and whether the world moves\n1191\tbetween utterances), so no single demand is separately identified, and the five-world reference interval bounds the subject-matter explanation without eliminating it. Its headline cells rest on 45\n1192\tconversations per system against 135 per enterprise cell, its reward carries an efficiency threshold\n1193\twhose sensitivity Table 9 bounds, and 215 of its\n1194\t450 conversations end at the step cap, with one\n1195\tprovider’s session cap censoring that system’s cells\n1196\t(Appendix E.1); the strongest cross-world claims\n1197\tare therefore restricted to the four metrics computed by identical code everywhere. Caller speech\n1198\tis synthesised rather than recorded, which weakens\n1199\t\n1200\t\fconclusions about accents and disfluency; the available real-speech corpora (Lin et al., 2026a; Zhao\n1201\tet al., 2026) do not provide goal-directed tasks\n1202\twith a verifiable end state, a limitation τ -Voice\n1203\tand EVA-Bench share (Ray et al., 2026; Bogavelli\n1204\tet al., 2026). The benchmark is English only, simulated users are more patient than real ones, tools are\n1205\tdeclarative mocks with zero latency, and the reward\n1206\tis binary with no partial credit. Our judge-based\n1207\tmetrics are assembled from instruments whose authors validated them against human ratings in their\n1208\tsource settings (Bogavelli et al., 2026; Reddy et al.,\n1209\t2022; Saeki et al., 2022; Mittag et al., 2021); we\n1210\thave not re-validated them on this corpus, so the associations we report are between our metrics rather\n1211\tthan between a metric and a human, and GS is\n1212\tpassable by inaction wherever the gold terminal\n1213\tstate equals the seeded state. An earlier version of\n1214\tthe benchmark included a seventh world; we cut\n1215\tit during task-design iteration and record the cut\n1216\trather than let a reader assume the design space is\n1217\tcovered by these six.\n1218\t\n1219\tEthics Statement\n1220\tPotential risks. A benchmark that ranks commercial voice agents can be used to justify deploying\n1221\tone on consequential work. Two of our results argue directly against reading it that way: metrics\n1222\ta system can win by declining to act do not track\n1223\tcapability, and the best-sounding systems in this\n1224\tstudy are not the most capable ones (Section 5).\n1225\tPerceived quality is not evidence of competence\n1226\there, and a procurement decision that uses one as\n1227\ta proxy for the other will select the wrong system.\n1228\tWe would rather this finding travel than the leaderboard.\n1229\tNo human subjects. Every caller utterance in\n1230\tD UPLEX W ORLD is synthesised; no human speech\n1231\twas recorded, solicited or replayed at any point, so\n1232\tno consent, compensation or institutional-review\n1233\tquestion arises. We state this explicitly because the\n1234\tbenchmark is about telephone conversations and a\n1235\treader is entitled to assume otherwise. The judgeand predictor-based instruments we use were validated against human ratings by their authors in\n1236\ttheir source settings (Bogavelli et al., 2026; Reddy\n1237\tet al., 2022; Saeki et al., 2022; Mittag et al., 2021).\n1238\tSynthetic personas are not a speaker population.\n1239\tThe personas carry accent, gender and speakingrate attributes, and systems do not perform equally\n1240\t\n1241\tacross them; Appendix I reports personas some\n1242\tconfigurations could not hear at all. Per-persona\n1243\tresults ship with the release so this is visible, but the\n1244\tpersona set represents no real population, and a perpersona gap in D UPLEX W ORLD is evidence about\n1245\tD UPLEX W ORLD’s personas rather than about the\n1246\tspeakers they resemble.\n1247\tAll task data is fabricated. Records, policies,\n1248\tidentifiers and transcripts are authored; no customer data or personal information of any kind\n1249\tenters the corpus, and the release pass strips any\n1250\tauthored value that collides with a real institution,\n1251\taddress or number space.\n1252\tCost and energy. Every number in this paper\n1253\tcomes from paid inference against commercial realtime APIs; the corpus totals 387 hours of simulated speech (Appendix P), so the scale of any\n1254\treproduction is known before it is incurred.\n1255\t\n1256\tReferences\n1257\tSiddhant Arora, Zhiyun Lu, Chung-Cheng Chiu, Ruoming Pang, and Shinji Watanabe. 2025. Talking turns:\n1258\tBenchmarking audio foundation models on turntaking dynamics. In International Conference on\n1259\tLearning Representations (ICLR).\n1260\tVictor Barres, Honghua Dong, Soham Ray, Xujie Si,\n1261\tand Karthik Narasimhan. 2025. τ 2 -bench: Evaluating conversational agents in a dual-control environment. Preprint, arXiv:2506.07982.\n1262\tTara Bogavelli, Gabrielle Gauthier Melançon, Katrina\n1263\tStankiewicz, Oluwanifemi Bamgbose, Fanny Riols,\n1264\tHoang H. Nguyen, Raghav Mehndiratta, Lindsay Devon Brin, Joseph Marinier, Hari Subramani, Anil\n1265\tMadamala, Sridhar Krishna Nemala, and Srinivas\n1266\tSunkara. 2026. EVA-Bench: A new end-to-end\n1267\tframework for evaluating voice agents. Preprint,\n1268\tarXiv:2605.13841.\n1269\tCarlos E. Jimenez, John Yang, Alexander Wettig,