hen capped runs are excluded rather than scored zero.\n2659\t\n2660\tM\n2661\t\n2662\tDuplex phenomenon audit\n2663\t\n2664\tWe print this audit rather than leave the claim on\n2665\tthe title page unsupported: a benchmark’s honest\n2666\tdescription includes the gap between what it elicits\n2667\tand what it scores. The harness elicits the fullduplex phenomena (user-side barge-in at a fixed\n2668\tcadence, backchannels, overlapping speech, dead\n2669\tair, and mid-utterance revision) and the suite scores\n2670\ta subset: TT scores every floor transfer’s offset\n2671\tand zeroes on unanswered turns, SEL scores the response to every injected distractor, and the SUSPEN SION type scores dead air across the gap. Elicited\n2672\tbut not scored: backchannel quality and placement,\n2673\tthe prosody of overlapping speech, and whether an\n2674\tagent’s mid-utterance revision is fluent or abrupt.\n2675\tThe reward never sees any of these, so a system\n2676\tcould be graceless at every overlap and lose nothing on the agentic pillar, which is why dynamics\n2677\tstays a pillar beside the reward rather than folded\n2678\tinto it, and why no composite is formed.\n2679\t\n2680\t\fN\n2681\t\n2682\tConversation-type excerpts: failure\n2683\tmodes\n2684\t\n2685\tO Enterprise results by conversation type\n2686\tTable 18 decomposes the enterprise deep dive of\n2687\tTable 13 by conversation type.\n2688\t\n2689\tP\n2690\t\n2691\tRun statistics\n2692\t\n2693\tThe scored corpus comprises 3,825 conversations\n2694\t(3,375 enterprise, 450 Pathfinding), totalling 387\n2695\thours of simulated speech. The Pathfinding block\n2696\tis 9 campaigned scenarios × 5 systems × 5 runs\n2697\t× 2 channels, median duration 20.0 minutes; 215\n2698\tof its 450 conversations end at the step cap and\n2699\tnone at the wall-clock cap (Appendix E.1), and its\n2700\trealistic channel carries the headline cells (225 conversations). In the enterprise worlds, 302 conversations end at the wall-clock timeout; every capped\n2701\tor timed-out run scores reward 0 and remains in\n2702\tthe corpus (Section 4.2).\n2703\t\n2704\tQ\n2705\t\n2706\tResults in pictures\n2707\t\n2708\tFigures 7–10 redraw the headline numbers of Section 5 for visual comparison.\n2709\t\n2710\tR\n2711\t\n2712\tLicense of Artifacts\n2713\t\n2714\tAll artifacts are used for non-commercial academic research, consistent with their terms. The\n2715\tfive evaluated systems are proprietary services accessed through their providers’ APIs under the\n2716\tcorresponding terms of service: Nova 2 Sonic\n2717\tvia Amazon Bedrock, Gemini-3.1-Flash-Live via\n2718\tthe Google Gemini API, GPT-Realtime-2.1 and\n2719\tGPT-Realtime-2.1-mini via the OpenAI API, and\n2720\tGrok Voice Think Fast 1.0 via the xAI API. The\n2721\tharness-side models are likewise proprietary services: the user simulator gpt-5.6-luna via Azure,\n2722\tand the decision model claude-haiku-4.5 and\n2723\tthe judge models (gpt-5.2, claude-opus-4.6,\n2724\tgrok-4.3) via OpenRouter under each provider’s\n2725\tterms; caller and walker speech is synthesised\n2726\tthrough the ElevenLabs API under its terms. No\n2727\tprovider model weights are redistributed. Among\n2728\topen-source evaluation components, the τ -bench\n2729\tand τ 2 -bench harnesses our orchestrator descends\n2730\tfrom are MIT-licensed, as is the DNSMOS code\n2731\tin the DNS-Challenge repository and the UTMOS\n2732\t(UTMOS22) system; NISQA is MIT-licensed code\n2733\twith pretrained weights under CC BY-NC-SA 4.0,\n2734\twhich our non-commercial use respects; the turntaking metric is reimplemented from EVA-Bench’s\n2735\t\n2736\tpublished specification (Bogavelli et al., 2026).\n2737\tTelephony coding follows the public ITU-T G.711\n2738\tstandard. All scenarios, records, policies, personas\n2739\tand identifiers in D UPLEX W ORLD are authored by\n2740\tus and contain no third-party data, and the realistic\n2741\tchannel’s background-noise recording inventory is\n2742\tdistributed with the release. We release the scenario corpus, annotations and evaluation code for\n2743\tresearch use, under CC BY 4.0 for data and MIT\n2744\tfor code.\n2745\t\n2746\tS\n2747\t\n2748\tUse of AI Assistants\n2749\t\n2750\tAI assistants were used in the writing and coding\n2751\tprocess, and not as part of the research itself. In\n2752\twriting, assistance was limited to grammar and\n2753\tspell checking. In coding, an AI-assisted IDE\n2754\t(Claude Code) was used during implementation.\n2755\tTask design, scenario authoring, experiments, analyses and conclusions are the authors’ own.\n2756\t\n2757\t\fFigure 6: Characteristic failure modes: the failing analogues of Figure 2, drawn from failing runs of the scored\n2758\tcorpus, with the mechanism of each failure stated beneath the excerpt. The two Pathfinding tiles are the same task\n2759\tinstances as their successes in Figure 2.\n2760\t\n2761\tmetric\n2762\t\n2763\tsystem\n2764\t2S\n2765\t3.1\n2766\t\n2767\tPass@1\n2768\t\n2769\t2.1\n2770\tmini\n2771\tVTF\n2772\t2S\n2773\t3.1\n2774\t\n2775\tACT\n2776\t\n2777\t2.1\n2778\tmini\n2779\tVTF\n2780\t2S\n2781\t3.1\n2782\t\n2783\tNLA\n2784\t\n2785\t2.1\n2786\tmini\n2787\tVTF\n2788\t2S\n2789\t3.1\n2790\t\n2791\tno-tool\n2792\t\n2793\t2.1\n2794\tmini\n2795\tVTF\n2796\t2S\n2797\t\n2798\tWERcred\n2799\t(%)\n2800\t\n2801\t3.1\n2802\t2.1\n2803\tmini\n2804\tVTF\n2805\t\n2806\t0.000\n2807\t0.493\n2808\t0.640\n2809\t0.320\n2810\t0.680\n2811\t\n2812\t0.000\n2813\t0.320\n2814\t0.533\n2815\t0.107\n2816\t0.493\n2817\t\n2818\t0.000\n2819\t0.360\n2820\t0.573\n2821\t0.160\n2822\t0.453\n2823\t\n2824\t0.000\n2825\t0.347\n2826\t0.440\n2827\t0.267\n2828\t0.360\n2829\t\n2830\t0.120\n2831\t0.307\n2832\t0.507\n2833\t0.227\n2834\t0.413\n2835\t\n2836\t0.000\n2837\t0.440\n2838\t0.413\n2839\t0.187\n2840\t0.467\n2841\t\n2842\t0.000\n2843\t0.467\n2844\t0.440\n2845\t0.200\n2846\t0.667\n2847\t\n2848\t0.000\n2849\t0.320\n2850\t0.333\n2851\t0.107\n2852\t0.440\n2853\t\n2854\t0.000\n2855\t0.360\n2856\t0.440\n285